WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.540
And now, I'd like to introduce you to our speakers today,

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.345
Professor Rita Popat and Professor Kristin Sainani.

00:00:17.345 --> 00:00:19.580
So, Professor Rita Popat is

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.660
a clinical associate professor in the Department of Health Research and Policy,

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.780
and the co-director of the master's program in

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.285
Clinical Research in Epidemiology at Stanford University.

00:00:29.285 --> 00:00:32.270
She has a Ph.D. in epidemiology and also holds

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:36.170
master's degrees in Biostatistics and Physical Therapy.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.990
Rita has taught several methodology and

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:42.829
data analysis courses at Stanford for more than a decade,

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:46.340
and has received several Excellence in Teaching and Mentoring Awards from

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.585
the Graduate Program in Epidemiology and at the Stanford Medical School.

00:00:50.585 --> 00:00:55.940
Dr. Popat specializes in teaching and mentoring medical and allied health professionals.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.805
She has a strong interest in developing curriculum

00:00:58.805 --> 00:01:01.700
and implementing new instruction models and recently,

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.290
developed a blended course in Epidemiology

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.235
and Biostatistics for Stanford medical students.

00:01:07.235 --> 00:01:11.570
She is also interested in neurodegenerative diseases and studies the role of

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:18.000
genetic and environmental factors in Parkinson's disease and lateral sclerosis.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.145
c So, Professor Kristin Sainani is an associate professor here at Stanford University.

00:01:23.145 --> 00:01:28.230
She also co-directs Stanford's Ph.D. program in Epidemiology and Clinical Research.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:32.820
Kristin teaches statistics and writing and performs research in sports medicine.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.245
She also writes about statistics and science for a range of different audiences.

00:01:37.245 --> 00:01:40.740
Professor Sainani has received several teaching awards,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.840
including Stanford's 2018 Biosciences Award for Excellence in Graduate Teaching.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.030
She has authored the health column body news for Allure magazine for a decade,

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.150
and she is also the statistical  editor for the journal,

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.680
Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.075
and she authors a statistics column,

00:01:58.075 --> 00:02:00.355
Statistically Speaking for this Journal.

00:02:00.355 --> 00:02:04.140
She teaches two popular Massive Open Online courses,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.840
including Statistics in Medicine on OpenEdX,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.335
and Writing in the Sciences on OpenEdX and Coursera.

00:02:10.335 --> 00:02:13.770
And now, I'd like to turn the floor over to Kristin and Rita.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.870
Thank you. Well, welcome everyone.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.600
Good to have you all.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.010
what I'm hoping to do in

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:24.000
the next few minutes today is to give you an idea of the kind of

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.925
content and material you're likely to

00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:31.400
see in the certificate program in Clinical Research and Epidemiology.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.440
So, just briefly, when we talk about clinical research and epi,

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.100
what we're often interested in is

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.560
an understanding or evaluating a relationship between an exposure and an outcome.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.120
And the exposure could be anything,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.645
it could be a genetic factor,

00:02:45.645 --> 00:02:48.245
a non genetic factor, a drug,

00:02:48.245 --> 00:02:51.450
or any sort of lifestyle factor.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:56.010
Here are three examples that just hit the news last week.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.810
I researched this in preparation for today's webinar.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.275
Here's the first one.

00:03:01.275 --> 00:03:03.975
Does alcohol consumption during breastfeeding,

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:07.785
is that associated with poor cognitive outcomes in children?

00:03:07.785 --> 00:03:11.340
Another example that we saw recently and last week,

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.525
was actually researchers here at Stanford,

00:03:13.525 --> 00:03:16.800
where they examined whether a Acetyl-L-Carnitine,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.210
I'm just going to call it LAC,

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.810
whether these levels

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.390
and this is a chemical, by the way,

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:22.570
which we all produce,

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.190
naturally in the body,

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:27.540
and it's important for brain function.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.140
and they wanted to see if this was associated with

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.450
increased risk of major depressive disorder,

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.595
which I will just refer to as depression.

00:03:35.595 --> 00:03:37.725
And here's a third example.

00:03:37.725 --> 00:03:40.530
This is sort of a stem cell therapy example,

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.380
where do induced pluripotent stem cells,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.910
can they help

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.430
cure, so to speak,

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:47.640
or reduce the symptoms,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.505
alleviate the symptoms in Parkinson's disease?

00:03:50.505 --> 00:03:51.930
As you can see here,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.710
we've got three examples of three very different kinds of exposures.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.390
We've got alcohol consumption, which is, of course,

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:00.120
a lifestyle factor,

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.060
it is an exogenous sort of risk factor.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.015
We've got a naturally

00:04:05.015 --> 00:04:07.420
endogenous chemical like the LAC,

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.200
and then we've got sort of stem cell therapy.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.950
Let's elaborate a little bit.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:15.745
Let's look at this example with LAC levels in MDD.

00:04:15.745 --> 00:04:18.400
As I said, these are researchers from Stanford.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:22.820
The study was published in the Proceedings of National Academy of Sciences.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.970
This got picked up,

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.240
got a lot of news attention,

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.610
you can see there's one article talking about whether low LAC levels is a new biomarker.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.830
With this new interest in precision medicine and

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.860
the role of biomarkers in

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:41.130
predicting who's at risk for certain diseases.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:46.179
This is sort of an interesting angle to look at this particular chemical.

00:04:46.179 --> 00:04:49.650
Then you've got a blood test that could be used for treatment.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.030
So, you've got a prediction

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.935
potential as well as a treatment potential.

00:04:54.935 --> 00:04:59.610
Very briefly, what the researchers did is they looked at LAC levels in

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:04.620
the plasma among a group of patients who had MDD and a group that didn't.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.670
The blue dots are the ones who don't have major depressive disorders and

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:13.185
the red dots stand for people who actually do have major depressive disorder.

00:05:13.185 --> 00:05:16.130
And you can see that LAC levels are

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:20.640
lower in those with MDD compared to those who don't have it.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.829
And we won't to get into the statistics,

00:05:22.829 --> 00:05:29.480
but you can see it's a highly statistically significant p-value of .0002.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.375
You might say, "Oh, wow. "

00:05:31.375 --> 00:05:33.285
Should you be compelled?

00:05:33.285 --> 00:05:37.300
Should we all go out and start taking the supplement which,

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.750
by the way, is available,

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:42.045
in health food stores?

00:05:42.045 --> 00:05:47.570
Would you be recommending this to your relatives or friends who may have MDD?

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.259
Hm.

00:05:49.259 --> 00:05:52.755
I hope you all will be skeptical because

00:05:52.755 --> 00:05:56.970
before we see and decide whether we should start taking this

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:59.460
supplement, we want to ask the question,

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:04.460
and this is what we hope to train everybody who's in our program to first ask the question,

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:10.050
how was this association between LAC levels and MDD evaluated?

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.575
We logically think of this as three steps.

00:06:13.575 --> 00:06:16.275
The first step is a research methodology.

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:18.310
Then we go to step two,

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:20.830
which is how was the analysis done?

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:24.070
And finally, how do we make

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:28.030
our interpretation based on the results and how the study was conducted?

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:32.040
So, let me elaborate a little bit on step one, since my colleague,

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:34.390
Dr. Sainani, will elaborate on step two,

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:36.765
which is the statistical analysis.

00:06:36.765 --> 00:06:37.970
So, in step one,

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:40.275
when we talk about research methodology,

00:06:40.275 --> 00:06:43.255
simply put, we're asking three basic things.

00:06:43.255 --> 00:06:45.100
How was the study done?

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.015
What was the study design?

00:06:47.015 --> 00:06:48.480
Who was in the study?

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.425
What was the study population?

00:06:50.425 --> 00:06:53.510
And finally, what measurements were made?

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:56.740
I want you to think about,

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:02.165
if you were interested in this relationship between LAC levels and MDD,

00:07:02.165 --> 00:07:06.140
what do you think might be an appropriate way to actually design the study?

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:09.695
I'm going to leave out the clinical trial because

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:13.400
we're not gonna start randomizing people.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.985
We first wanna see evidence from observational studies.

00:07:16.985 --> 00:07:19.595
Let me help you out.

00:07:19.595 --> 00:07:21.340
I'm sure you all

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.710
can probably come up with this design.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:25.690
Let's say you're the investigator.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:29.400
You're going to get a group of people who

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.085
don't have MDD now.

00:07:32.085 --> 00:07:33.580
We won't get into inclusion,

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:37.015
exclusion criteria, but you will choose your cohort of people.

00:07:37.015 --> 00:07:40.640
You will then measure their LAC levels at baseline,

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.270
and then you will follow them forward in time,

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.165
and then you'll evaluate who develops MDD versus not.

00:07:46.165 --> 00:07:50.515
Sorry guys, I know the guys lying flat are not meant to be dead.

00:07:50.515 --> 00:07:52.240
It's just to

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:53.940
illustrate the two different groups,

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.735
a group that developed MDD and a group that didn't.

00:07:56.735 --> 00:07:58.570
By the way, a fancy word

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.380
for this design is a prospective cohort study.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:02.790
We like these studies

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:06.925
and you can see why because causality's in the right direction.

00:08:06.925 --> 00:08:11.440
What I mean by that is that the exposure that is LAC levels comes first,

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.835
followed by the outcome MDD.

00:08:13.835 --> 00:08:16.350
If we do find an association,

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:20.565
at least the design helps us explore whether this is causal in nature.

00:08:20.565 --> 00:08:23.635
As I mentioned, that's a fancy term for this design.

00:08:23.635 --> 00:08:27.330
Now here's another way to do it and maybe some of you came up with this design.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:29.360
So, you're still the investigator,

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.620
but now you start with a group of people who have MDD,

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.010
and a group of people who don't have MDD.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:38.020
And then you go back and then you try to determine

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:41.955
their LAC levels either taken at that cross-section in time,

00:08:41.955 --> 00:08:44.785
or maybe from samples that were stored previously.

00:08:44.785 --> 00:08:46.430
Now let me ask you this,

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:48.450
among these two designs--

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:50.440
By the way, this is the case control study.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.025
Of these two designs,

00:08:52.025 --> 00:08:54.965
which one do you think is the stronger one to,

00:08:54.965 --> 00:08:59.725
bear more credibility that there is a causal link?

00:08:59.725 --> 00:09:02.900
I think we would all say the prospective cohort study.

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:07.615
What if I told you what these researchers did was actually a case control study?

00:09:07.615 --> 00:09:10.839
You see before you get all starry-eyed

00:09:10.839 --> 00:09:14.380
and blown away by the statistics and the p-value,

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:17.990
you really have to evaluate how the study was done.

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:22.430
Now, I'm not trying to say that the researchers say that there is a causal link.

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:28.215
I want to be mindful that they were correct in the way they interpreted the data.

00:09:28.215 --> 00:09:29.850
But for most of us,

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:31.160
when we look at the literature,

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:36.370
we just focus on the statistical piece and the P values,

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:41.155
and less so on what the methods were that generated these results.

00:09:41.155 --> 00:09:42.900
And in my courses,

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.030
I focus a lot on this methodology piece

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:48.450
because we can see that certain study designs lend

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:51.760
themselves better to understanding causal associations.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:54.570
We also want to ask other questions.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.240
So this is about measurements.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:59.195
How was Major Depressive Disorder ascertained?

00:09:59.195 --> 00:10:01.585
Were reliable and valid methods used?

00:10:01.585 --> 00:10:07.335
Was it used in a standardized manner in the entire study sample?

00:10:07.335 --> 00:10:09.110
How were the LAC level measures?

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:10.720
Were the assays reliable?

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:12.090
How about validity?

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:17.695
Does a LAC level taken at one cross-section of time,

00:10:17.695 --> 00:10:20.120
is that truly reflective of one's,

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:22.905
exposure to these LAC levels over time?

00:10:22.905 --> 00:10:26.220
You see it raises a ton of questions which we need to keep in

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:30.320
mind in order to correctly interpret the study findings,

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.460
and then because it's an observational study,

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:35.435
we worry about other confounders as well.

00:10:35.435 --> 00:10:42.700
What we have to really be able to separate is causation versus association.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:46.520
We can't see non causal explanations that can

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:50.790
generate spurious associations which were then exposure and outcome.

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:56.180
In this course we also spend time talking about sources of study biases that can

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:59.150
produce these furious associations when actually there's

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:02.695
nothing going on between the exposure and the outcome.

00:11:02.695 --> 00:11:07.545
We first have to actually rule out all non-causal explanations,

00:11:07.545 --> 00:11:10.335
and then ask the question

00:11:10.335 --> 00:11:15.255
is there evidence to support a causal association between the exposure and outcome?

00:11:15.255 --> 00:11:17.575
And two quick points.

00:11:17.575 --> 00:11:20.630
Just because you absorb a statistically or

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:24.305
clinically significant effect doesn't mean there is a real effect.

00:11:24.305 --> 00:11:26.210
And the converse is also true.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:31.250
Just because you fail to observe a statistically or a clinically significant effect,

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:34.310
it doesn't mean that a real effect is absent.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:39.380
The study methodology could be so flawed that you actually fail to pick up the signal.

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:44.275
A flawed study methodology can also do,

00:11:44.275 --> 00:11:45.690
what we see above,

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:48.215
where it can produce a spurious effect.

00:11:48.215 --> 00:11:53.085
We kind of have to be detectives, clinical research detectives,

00:11:53.085 --> 00:11:55.450
and as detectives we do is

00:11:55.450 --> 00:12:01.825
we try to figure out how much was a study finding influenced by bias.

00:12:01.825 --> 00:12:03.005
And what's bias?

00:12:03.005 --> 00:12:06.335
It's any deviation of truth beyond chance error.

00:12:06.335 --> 00:12:10.265
These are systematic errors by the investigators in sampling,

00:12:10.265 --> 00:12:12.970
collecting, or interpreting data.

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:17.010
This is how we culminate the course where

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:22.190
we've learnt about the methodology and I give students papers to read, they read it.

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:24.010
We look at the study findings.

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:27.945
If we believe that the study has some truth in it,

00:12:27.945 --> 00:12:30.745
then we say the study has internal validity,

00:12:30.745 --> 00:12:33.375
and then we move on and we ask,

00:12:33.375 --> 00:12:36.050
could this be useful in clinical practice or in

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:39.625
designing the next research question?

00:12:39.625 --> 00:12:44.640
On the other hand, if we determine that there's too much bias in this study

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:47.635
and that it really affects the validity

00:12:47.635 --> 00:12:51.150
and we are skeptical whether there's truth true in this study,

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:53.095
we stop right there.

00:12:53.095 --> 00:12:55.245
But in the previous scenario,

00:12:55.245 --> 00:12:57.345
if the study does have some truth,

00:12:57.345 --> 00:13:00.770
then you can ask about generalizability or external validity.

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:02.620
And this is where we ask the question,

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:09.220
can what we found in our sample be applicable to the study population beyond that?

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:12.070
and where we want to apply these findings do.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:15.800
But if we messed up the way we sampled and the way we actually

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:19.905
study then came up with the study sample that generated the results,

00:13:19.905 --> 00:13:22.520
then while the study may be valid,

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:25.430
it may not be generalizable to the population

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:28.875
beyond that was included in that particular study.

00:13:28.875 --> 00:13:33.120
So, true or false? I get this question a lot because not everybody is going to

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:37.245
be doing perspective data collection and be designing these studies

00:13:37.245 --> 00:13:39.455
and you may be working with existing data.

00:13:39.455 --> 00:13:41.370
Here's the question I get.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:44.240
We don't need to worry about study design and measurements because we're

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:47.490
using an existing database or a datasource

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:49.610
and information has already been collected.

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:53.090
We just need to understand how to do the data analysis.

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:56.625
Of course, I'm setting you up because this is false,

00:13:56.625 --> 00:13:58.620
and I hope we've given you,

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:03.320
I've been able to give you a small example to show you why it's not.

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:06.285
Even if it's just secondary data that you're using,

00:14:06.285 --> 00:14:10.450
you still within that need to ask yourself about measurements,

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:13.080
about whether it's the right database to be

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:15.940
answering a particular exposure outcome relationship,

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:21.573
and whether you can even get to answering the question about causation.

00:14:21.573 --> 00:14:22.955
In a nutshell,

00:14:22.955 --> 00:14:26.875
when we want to examine an exposure outcome relationship,

00:14:26.875 --> 00:14:29.910
step one is research methodology.

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:32.330
The statistics is very important too because

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:35.815
Epidemiology and Clinical Research is a quantitative field,

00:14:35.815 --> 00:14:39.150
so you have the methods and you need the statistics and

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:41.510
analytic approaches to be able to

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:45.150
quantify the relationship between the exposure and outcome.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:50.560
But the methodology informs the statistics and in this series,

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:52.305
in this certificate program too,

00:14:52.305 --> 00:14:56.795
you will learn about statistical approaches that can actually help you

00:14:56.795 --> 00:15:00.930
account for some of the weaknesses in the study methodology,

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:05.040
things like confounding and control for those types of variables.

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:08.070
Finally, we need both the methodology and

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:11.960
the analysis to help us with the interpretation.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:16.580
So clinical research and epidemiology, for whom?

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:18.650
Who should be studying this?

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:24.820
If any of you are interested in research or studies that involve human subjects,

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:28.165
either active data collection or recorded data,

00:15:28.165 --> 00:15:30.610
this might be the one for you.

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:33.060
What skills will you learn?

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.685
These are just some that

00:15:34.685 --> 00:15:36.670
I've put here.

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:39.580
I'm sure there are others and Dr. Sainani can chime in,

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:43.115
but here are some of the skills that you hope students,

00:15:43.115 --> 00:15:44.290
not just in our program,

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:46.260
but in our certificate program will learn.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:49.560
How to critically evaluate a published paper?

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:53.745
How do you conduct peer review of a research manuscript or grant?

00:15:53.745 --> 00:15:56.580
Some of you may be serving in these roles and it's

00:15:56.580 --> 00:16:01.235
a huge contribution to the scientific community to know how to critically

00:16:01.235 --> 00:16:04.890
do a peer review of a manuscript or a grant,

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:08.080
which is all done on a volunteer basis by the way.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:12.530
How to develop a study protocol for an observational study or a clinical trial?

00:16:12.530 --> 00:16:14.440
For those of you who are in the trenches,

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:18.090
who are helping PIs design studies, conduct studies,

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:23.580
our courses will help you develop these type of study protocols to assist the PI

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:27.345
or perhaps you are the PI who's developing the protocol.

00:16:27.345 --> 00:16:32.505
To help you develop analytic approach appropriate for the proposed study design.

00:16:32.505 --> 00:16:34.132
To conduct data analysis.

00:16:34.132 --> 00:16:38.315
with the help of the courses that my colleague here Dr. Sainani teaches,

00:16:38.315 --> 00:16:41.010
and how to interpret results.

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:44.370
So very briefly, in our Masters program where we

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:47.120
have three core courses in the methodology series,

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:51.850
you've seen that the numbers here are HRP 225, 226, 251.

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:53.800
The description is given below.

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:55.595
In the certificate program,

00:16:55.595 --> 00:16:57.645
we are asking students to

00:16:57.645 --> 00:16:59.100
you're required to take two of them.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:01.675
That's 225 and 251.

00:17:01.675 --> 00:17:04.225
So, what do these courses cover?

00:17:04.225 --> 00:17:11.160
HRP 225, which is design and conduct of clinical and epidemiology research studies,

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.365
we cover descriptive epidemiology,

00:17:13.365 --> 00:17:16.870
sampling methods, reliability and validity of measurements,

00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:18.920
analytic study designs,

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:20.480
their strengths and limitations,

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:24.230
cohort case-control, cross-sectional studies.

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:26.930
We talk about measures of association,

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:28.520
things that you might be familiar with,

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:30.390
like odds ratios, risk ratios,

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:33.355
hazard ratios, sample size estimation.

00:17:33.355 --> 00:17:34.970
How do you power your studies?

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:38.230
How many people do you need to study a relationship?

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:40.915
Sources of bias, confounding.

00:17:40.915 --> 00:17:42.479
We talk about interactions.

00:17:42.479 --> 00:17:44.560
We talk about causal inference.

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:49.550
We do a systematic critique as a culmination having learned all of these concepts

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:53.010
and we also touch upon diagnostic tests and screening.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:54.890
The spring quarter class,

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:56.615
which is called HRP 251,

00:17:56.615 --> 00:18:00.085
is focused on clinical trials, exclusively.

00:18:00.085 --> 00:18:03.780
So, you must have an intervention that you can randomize.

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:06.880
Now we also touch upon early phase trials,

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:11.260
so let me just go through some of the topics that we cover in this course.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:13.490
We've got design issues,

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:16.010
how to think about outcomes and how to select them,

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:19.055
surrogate versus hard clinical endpoints.

00:18:19.055 --> 00:18:21.615
We discuss phase one, phase two studies,

00:18:21.615 --> 00:18:24.325
these are your early phase study designs,

00:18:24.325 --> 00:18:25.875
phase three study design,

00:18:25.875 --> 00:18:27.775
the large efficacy trials.

00:18:27.775 --> 00:18:30.274
We talk about recruitment and adherence.

00:18:30.274 --> 00:18:32.205
Sample size and power.

00:18:32.205 --> 00:18:34.015
Since we are at Stanford,

00:18:34.015 --> 00:18:38.650
and we have people who have developed amazing adaptive design.

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:44.350
You will hear about adaptive designs in designing your clinical trials.

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:46.205
How to handle missing data,

00:18:46.205 --> 00:18:48.510
data analysis, and interim monitoring,

00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:50.495
both from a statistical perspective,

00:18:50.495 --> 00:18:54.005
as well as the data monitoring committee perspective.

00:18:54.005 --> 00:18:56.135
So with that,

00:18:56.135 --> 00:18:57.825
I hope I've

00:18:57.825 --> 00:19:00.630
piqued your interest and your appetite for

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:02.040
clinical research in Epi

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:05.435
and now, to my wonderful colleague Dr. Sainani.

00:19:05.435 --> 00:19:09.720
Thanks, Dr. Popat. That was actually a great lead in,

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:11.170
to my presentation

00:19:11.170 --> 00:19:15.465
because my courses focus primarily on the statistics,

00:19:15.465 --> 00:19:18.580
but it's so important to keep the statistics in context.

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:20.030
How were the data collected?

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:21.565
Where did those data come from?

00:19:21.565 --> 00:19:22.710
You can't interpret that,

00:19:22.710 --> 00:19:24.550
the statistics in a vacuum.

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:27.020
I teach a three

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:30.010
course sequence in biostatistics

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:33.550
for the Master's program in Epidemiology and Clinical Research at Stanford.

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:35.480
You see the course numbers up there.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.330
For the certificate program,

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:39.910
you would take the first two of those courses, which is

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:44.580
my introductory and my intermediate biostatistics courses.

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:47.530
I'll just start by talking a little bit about,

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:49.320
something that Dr. Popat

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:54.255
alluded to which is that you have to be skeptical of the published literature.

00:19:54.255 --> 00:19:57.100
And we have, as many of you are probably aware,

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:59.765
a reproducibility crisis in science

00:19:59.765 --> 00:20:02.410
and this has to do with many facets as

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:04.125
Dr. Popat alluded to,

00:20:04.125 --> 00:20:08.220
research design, how you design the studies is a key part of that.

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:10.150
I'm going to focus, of course, more on the statistics,

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:12.940
but it's all important.

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:19.160
This is one of my favorite studies that came out in 2005 by John Ioannidis, who is,

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:22.695
actually now a faculty member in the Division of Epidemiology at Stanford

00:20:22.695 --> 00:20:27.300
and he published a paper called My Most Published Research Findings are False.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:31.680
And he was pointing out that a lot of the

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:35.420
findings in the literature actually are false positives,

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:40.145
spurious associations that can arise either because you do a study with bias,

00:20:40.145 --> 00:20:42.640
or because of the study design,

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:44.200
or because of the statistics,

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:46.135
or all three of those.

00:20:46.135 --> 00:20:49.685
If you like late-night television as I do,

00:20:49.685 --> 00:20:51.350
you may have caught

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:52.755
there was a John Oliver piece,

00:20:52.755 --> 00:20:53.915
a couple of years back

00:20:53.915 --> 00:20:55.435
just dealing with

00:20:55.435 --> 00:20:57.250
talking about this whole reproducibility crisis.

00:20:57.250 --> 00:20:59.700
So you see this even in the media

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:02.365
and the popular media right now

00:21:02.365 --> 00:21:04.240
there's been a couple of high-profile cases,

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:05.320
I'll just point out here.

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:08.240
So, there is a case with a scientist,

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:11.714
a high-profile scientist at Cornell who does food science.

00:21:11.714 --> 00:21:13.690
His research had been highly cited,

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:15.045
talked about in the media,

00:21:15.045 --> 00:21:19.210
and his research has come under scrutiny for a number of reasons

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:22.550
one of which is sloppy data handling, something we're gonna try to

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:24.270
talk about with you in my classes,

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:25.400
just try to help you to

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:27.255
to not have sloppy data handling.

00:21:27.255 --> 00:21:30.195
Another issue is

00:21:30.195 --> 00:21:32.380
a lot of his research findings

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.495
seem to have come out of what's called P hacking.

00:21:34.495 --> 00:21:36.305
What that means is

00:21:36.305 --> 00:21:38.335
if you push your data in enough ways,

00:21:38.335 --> 00:21:43.115
if you kind of try all possible associations with all the variables in your data set,

00:21:43.115 --> 00:21:46.175
you are going to find some spurious associations.

00:21:46.175 --> 00:21:47.735
Some things that

00:21:47.735 --> 00:21:50.895
achieve statistical significance, P is less than .05.

00:21:50.895 --> 00:21:53.300
But those are almost certainly going to be false positives

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:55.420
and that's one of the things we talk about in my classes

00:21:55.420 --> 00:21:57.985
is trying to avoid P hacking.

00:21:57.985 --> 00:21:59.885
I'll just put up a few more.

00:21:59.885 --> 00:22:04.390
This was an article that was in 538.com.

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:06.030
This was actually

00:22:06.030 --> 00:22:07.825
something I was involved in.

00:22:07.825 --> 00:22:10.020
There was a statistical method introduced into

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:14.005
the sports science literature that causes high false positives.

00:22:14.005 --> 00:22:19.245
Some of you may have read in New York Times or other media outlets about

00:22:19.245 --> 00:22:23.330
some famous research on power poses in psychology

00:22:23.330 --> 00:22:25.095
that's been largely discredited.

00:22:25.095 --> 00:22:28.335
Again, a lot of these things have to do with poor study design,

00:22:28.335 --> 00:22:31.980
as well as poorly done statistics.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:34.310
What I try to do in my classes

00:22:34.310 --> 00:22:36.950
is to teach you how to do reproducible research

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:38.550
and to do good statistics.

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:41.500
I blame some of the issues

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:45.530
with poor statistics in the literature on how we teach statistics.

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:48.640
And I'm not saying that all statistical teaching is bad.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:53.055
I think there are some great statistical teaching that goes on out there,

00:22:53.055 --> 00:22:58.565
but I notice that a lot of statistical teachings seems to fall in one or two camps.

00:22:58.565 --> 00:23:01.570
The first approach is a perfectly valid method.

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:03.640
This is how I was trained,

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.140
but it's highly theoretical

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:06.700
with a lot of math.

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:07.910
A lot of calculus,

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:09.630
linear algebra, and proofs.

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:13.230
Not so much practical training on how to deal with data.

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:15.120
I love this kind of stuff.

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:16.540
I like to do proofs,

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:20.755
one of those equations up there is from one of my papers from this year,

00:23:20.755 --> 00:23:24.510
but this approach is not appropriate for every audience.

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:30.185
People who are doing applied sciences in psychology, education, medicine,

00:23:30.185 --> 00:23:32.680
may not need to know all of the math,

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:35.140
and may not need to work out all the calculus

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:38.000
if their goal is really just to analyze your data properly.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:40.920
If you try to teach it in this way

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:42.470
and it's not a match with the audience,

00:23:42.470 --> 00:23:44.720
sometimes what ends up happening, I've noticed,

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:47.180
is that we swing to the other extreme.

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:49.545
Instead of teaching all the math,

00:23:49.545 --> 00:23:51.240
we suddenly teach none of the math

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:53.580
and you end up with more of a cookbook approach

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:56.630
and that's the approach that I find really problematic.

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:59.750
Because when you end up taking out all of the math,

00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:01.600
it becomes more of

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:02.715
I just have some data,

00:24:02.715 --> 00:24:06.440
I want to know how to get it in the statistical analysis program and get something out,

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:08.335
click the right buttons and get results.

00:24:08.335 --> 00:24:13.720
And I think this is a problematic way to teach statistics and also to do statistics.

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:17.835
It leads to some of the reproducibility issues that I was just talking about

00:24:17.835 --> 00:24:22.895
because if you are just kind of throwing your data into a statistical black box,

00:24:22.895 --> 00:24:25.260
not really understanding what's happening in that box,

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:28.270
and what the results mean that come out,

00:24:28.270 --> 00:24:31.865
unfortunately, this leads to all sorts of problems.

00:24:31.865 --> 00:24:34.090
And one of the problems is that

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:38.400
if you are kind of fuzzy on what exactly is coming out of that black box,

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:42.760
it's too easy to cherry-pick out of the results that come out.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:45.040
And I'm not saying scientists do this intentionally,

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.840
I think it's mostly unintentional,

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:49.920
but if you're kind of fuzzy on what those results mean,

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:52.160
it's very easy to just kind of select the ones that

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:55.190
seem to support the story that you want to tell

00:24:55.190 --> 00:25:00.375
and then get excited about that story and publish a story that is misleading.

00:25:00.375 --> 00:25:04.050
I'll just give you one example of this.

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:05.795
This is something I was looking at recently.

00:25:05.795 --> 00:25:08.470
This is from a published paper

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:11.690
and they were looking at glucosamine,

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:15.760
which is a supplement that sometimes people take for knee osteoarthritis,

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:19.770
which is pain and stiffness that you get in your knee as you grow older.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:23.450
And they randomly assigned- it was a randomized trial-

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:24.730
it was a smaller randomized trial,

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:26.600
I think it was about 50 people and they randomly

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:30.090
assigned half to take a glucosamine supplement daily,

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:32.065
and half to take a placebo pill.

00:25:32.065 --> 00:25:35.140
Now, if you looked at the published study,

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:37.980
there was no graphical representations of the data.

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:41.110
They simply put all the data in a table and I was looking

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:44.620
carefully at that table and noticed something and so

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:46.970
I do quite a bit of statistical sleuthing.

00:25:46.970 --> 00:25:49.720
As Rita said, we have to kind of be a detective and

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:52.610
I really enjoy statistical sleuthing.

00:25:52.610 --> 00:25:56.805
I actually just pulled the data out of the table and graphed it myself.

00:25:56.805 --> 00:25:59.255
There were two primary outcomes for this study.

00:25:59.255 --> 00:26:02.720
The pain that you have in your knees and your disease severity,

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:04.380
sort of an overall disease severity.

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:07.130
I'm going to show you my graphs.

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:10.820
This is the data that I just took out the tables and I simply graphed it.

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:14.330
And what you're seeing here is the graph for pain.

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:17.510
The Y axis is the percent change since baseline,

00:26:17.510 --> 00:26:20.155
you want it to go down because you want pain to get better.

00:26:20.155 --> 00:26:23.515
And the X axis is time in weeks.

00:26:23.515 --> 00:26:25.795
This was a 24 week study.

00:26:25.795 --> 00:26:27.585
The blue line is the treatment group,

00:26:27.585 --> 00:26:29.490
the red line is the placebo group.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:32.045
And you do not need to know any calculus,

00:26:32.045 --> 00:26:34.717
or have a degree in math or statistics I think

00:26:34.717 --> 00:26:37.075
to interpret this graph correctly.

00:26:37.075 --> 00:26:40.360
I think we can all look at that and agree that there

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:43.880
was maybe some small decrease in both groups.

00:26:43.880 --> 00:26:48.510
If anything, the placebo group went down more in pain than the treatment group.

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:50.740
And actually, those two lines are

00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:52.970
within the statistical margin of error of each other.

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:56.680
Really there's no difference showing up between those two groups.

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:59.830
The authors of the paper got that right as I'll show you in a minute.

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:01.810
They got the right conclusion with pain.

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:06.070
Where things went wrong is when it came to disease severity.

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:08.080
Some kind of scale people filled

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:10.685
out to say how bad their disease is flaring up.

00:27:10.685 --> 00:27:13.930
Again, we want it to be lower.

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:16.200
Take a second here and look at that graph,

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:19.000
same general graphic, treatment is in blue,

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:20.590
placebo is in red,

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:25.805
and take a second and see what conclusions you think you would draw from that graphic.

00:27:25.805 --> 00:27:29.970
I think all of us kind of looking at that would probably

00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:33.540
agree that it doesn't look like there's much difference between treatment and placebo.

00:27:33.540 --> 00:27:35.440
In fact, they ended up after 24 weeks,

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:39.230
at almost the same level of disease severity.

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:42.680
In fact, you can see there's some drop in disease severity.

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:45.490
And this indicates that there is some kind of placebo effect going on.

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:47.075
So, it's a subjective measure.

00:27:47.075 --> 00:27:48.555
People take a pill

00:27:48.555 --> 00:27:50.210
and when you take a pill sometimes,

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:52.260
you feel better just from the placebo effect.

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:53.945
That seems to be what we're seeing here.

00:27:53.945 --> 00:27:56.100
The only real difference that you can see between

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:59.935
the two groups is probably just due to chance fluctuation.

00:27:59.935 --> 00:28:01.740
They have a slightly different trajectory.

00:28:01.740 --> 00:28:03.200
For some reason,

00:28:03.200 --> 00:28:04.370
it might be just chance,

00:28:04.370 --> 00:28:08.195
the treatment group went up a little before it went down and then kind of was levelled,

00:28:08.195 --> 00:28:11.655
whereas the placebo group went down a little and then up and then went down,

00:28:11.655 --> 00:28:13.770
but they ended up in the same place.

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:19.010
So, it might surprise you to hear that what I would consider a null result,

00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:22.685
in the paper, the authors put a positive spin on it.

00:28:22.685 --> 00:28:25.410
And in fact, this is comes right out the abstract.

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:29.560
They say, these results suggest that the consumption of glucosamine,

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:34.635
the supplement, for 12 weeks or longer has a positive effect on osteoarthritis score.

00:28:34.635 --> 00:28:38.445
Osteoarthritis score is that disease severity score I was just showing you.

00:28:38.445 --> 00:28:39.670
Now, they've got it right in pain.

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:42.100
In contrast, it does not reduce pain.

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:45.240
So, let's look at that graphic again.

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:47.945
How in the world is that a positive effect?

00:28:47.945 --> 00:28:51.300
The placebo group and the treatment group basically did the same.

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:52.970
It doesn't seem to be any benefit from

00:28:52.970 --> 00:28:55.490
treatment and I think it's easy to see in the graphic.

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:57.320
So, what happened here?

00:28:57.320 --> 00:29:01.020
I think this is one of those cases where the authors took their data up,

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:02.800
they stuck it in a computer program,

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:06.540
they were happy to get out some result and in the midst of all those results,

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.420
there were a few significant P value.

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:10.465
And I'll tell you there was,

00:29:10.465 --> 00:29:12.660
a significant P value.

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:15.490
The drop from 4 weeks to 12 weeks in

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:18.575
the treatment group happened to be a significant decline.

00:29:18.575 --> 00:29:21.560
But guess what? The drop in the placebo group from

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:25.250
12 weeks to 24 weeks was also a significant decline.

00:29:25.250 --> 00:29:27.050
They didn't make a story out of that,

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:29.330
but there was a true decline here.

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:31.010
There was an actual placebo effect.

00:29:31.010 --> 00:29:33.340
People did feel a little better, subjectively.

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:36.080
There was also a significant P value,

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:38.520
there's a technique called repeated measures in Nova.

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:40.680
And when you plug those data into that,

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:42.815
you do get a significant P value.

00:29:42.815 --> 00:29:47.445
That significant P value though just tells you that the trajectory,

00:29:47.445 --> 00:29:51.560
that the lines, the path those two groups took was a little bit different,

00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:53.055
as you can see visually here.

00:29:53.055 --> 00:29:57.355
It does not tell you however that the treatment group beat the placebo group.

00:29:57.355 --> 00:30:00.790
Unfortunately, if you're not quite sure what these statistical methods are doing,

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:02.760
you'll see those significant P values as you get excited,

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:05.520
you make a story that's false.

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:10.950
I'm going to teach you in my courses some principles of effective statistics.

00:30:10.950 --> 00:30:13.020
One of the most important things

00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:15.995
is that you can't do statistics well without

00:30:15.995 --> 00:30:19.900
understanding the basics of probability and statistical inference.

00:30:19.900 --> 00:30:22.825
You can't do statistics without any math.

00:30:22.825 --> 00:30:25.555
Now, that doesn't mean we have to swing to the extreme

00:30:25.555 --> 00:30:28.350
of putting in a ton of calculus and linear algebra.

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:31.250
I believe that most of what you need to know in

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:35.470
statistics can be taught with subtraction, multiplication, division.

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:39.390
You may also need to be familiar with like decimals and fractions,

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:42.815
but that's the level of math that's required in my classes.

00:30:42.815 --> 00:30:45.345
You also need to use common sense around numbers.

00:30:45.345 --> 00:30:49.510
I'm going try to teach you what is statistical inference, what is probability,

00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:51.600
and a good strong understanding of those

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:54.100
but you don't need the fancy math necessarily for that.

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:58.000
And one of the techniques that I use for teaching is computer simulation.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:00.170
We will do a lot of computer simulation.

00:31:00.170 --> 00:31:02.025
I'll just give you an example of that.

00:31:02.025 --> 00:31:05.605
There's a lot of problems that can be solved mathematically,

00:31:05.605 --> 00:31:07.610
but can also be solved in a computer.

00:31:07.610 --> 00:31:11.120
And one of the classic probability problems that you're going to see in

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:15.300
any sort of probability 101 course is called the perfect problem.

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:16.760
And it goes something like this,

00:31:16.760 --> 00:31:18.775
some of you may have heard of it before.

00:31:18.775 --> 00:31:21.660
Imagine you have a class of 30 students.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:25.240
What's the probability that in a class of 30 students,

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:28.630
that two people, at least two people are going to share the same birthday?

00:31:28.630 --> 00:31:31.840
They were both born on August seventh.

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:33.820
And instinctively, most of us

00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:35.920
think that that probability is gonna be pretty small, right?

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:39.220
There's thirty people and there's 365 different birthdays.

00:31:39.220 --> 00:31:42.980
Um, surprisingly though, that probability is quite high.

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:44.475
In a class of 30,

00:31:44.475 --> 00:31:48.825
there's actually a 70 percent probability that two people will share a birthday.

00:31:48.825 --> 00:31:53.180
You can solve that problem mathematically,

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:55.050
and for those of you who are interested I will

00:31:55.050 --> 00:31:57.585
solve it mathematically in my course for you.

00:31:57.585 --> 00:32:00.330
But you can also solve that problem with

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:04.025
computer simulation and I think the computer simulation is really intuitive.

00:32:04.025 --> 00:32:07.290
And what you do, we have great computing power these days so,

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:09.505
you can do this on a laptop in a second,

00:32:09.505 --> 00:32:17.650
you actually have the computer randomly sample 30 birthdays out of all possible 365,

00:32:17.650 --> 00:32:20.630
366 if you include leap years birthdays,

00:32:20.630 --> 00:32:25.690
and you have a computer do that arbitrarily large number of times.

00:32:25.690 --> 00:32:27.050
So hundred thousand times,

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:28.935
a computer can do this in a second.

00:32:28.935 --> 00:32:32.220
You look then at all of those samples and you

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:35.460
look at the birthdays and see how many of those samples was there a match?

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:39.180
You had the computer count up and all those different 30 classrooms,

00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:41.660
out of 100,000 times, how many times did you get a match?

00:32:41.660 --> 00:32:46.085
And that would give you the exact same answer as if you solve it mathematically.

00:32:46.085 --> 00:32:48.540
We'll do a lot of computer simulation to help

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:52.420
motivate what's happening under the hood in statistics.

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:55.625
Another principle of effective statistics

00:32:55.625 --> 00:32:57.810
is you need to understand your data.

00:32:57.810 --> 00:33:00.250
We don't just stuff things into

00:33:00.250 --> 00:33:03.890
the statistical black box and press the button to do a t-test.

00:33:03.890 --> 00:33:05.420
Before you do that,

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:09.190
90 percent of your data analysis is actually not doing the t-test,

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:11.485
or the linear regression, or getting P values.

00:33:11.485 --> 00:33:14.100
It's actually drawing pictures with your data,

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:16.315
understanding how much missing data you have,

00:33:16.315 --> 00:33:18.720
understanding where your data came from,

00:33:18.720 --> 00:33:21.750
making sure that you don't have errors in your data,

00:33:21.750 --> 00:33:24.330
and really just getting a sense of what's going on in

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:27.605
your data before you ever run a formal statistical test.

00:33:27.605 --> 00:33:30.315
I've already talked about drawing lots of picture of your data.

00:33:30.315 --> 00:33:32.305
You need to use common sense.

00:33:32.305 --> 00:33:35.240
Always consider the totality of the evidence,

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:36.645
don't just cherry pick.

00:33:36.645 --> 00:33:40.115
And we're also going to talk a lot about keeping an accurate record

00:33:40.115 --> 00:33:44.390
in code of everything you do to the data in all the analyses you do.

