I would say it's

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:17.565
very rigorous in the sense

00:48:17.565 --> 00:48:20.660
that I want to start in the foundations and I want you

00:48:20.660 --> 00:48:24.450
to do data analysis properly so you're going to document your code,

00:48:24.450 --> 00:48:26.350
you're going to understand the models.

00:48:26.350 --> 00:48:29.610
My HRP 259 course largely focuses on that,

00:48:29.610 --> 00:48:33.127
so we don't necessarily get beyond regression analysis.

00:48:33.127 --> 00:48:35.660
We focus on doing statistics right.

00:48:35.660 --> 00:48:38.650
And I actually think that once you have that,

00:48:38.650 --> 00:48:44.480
the particular statistical test that you're going to run that's easy to look up online,

00:48:44.480 --> 00:48:47.000
and figure out how to do it in ourselves.

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:49.240
I think the foundational stuff is much more important.

00:48:49.240 --> 00:48:51.310
In my second

00:48:51.310 --> 00:48:54.210
course, we cover some more advanced topics.

00:48:54.210 --> 00:48:59.260
We don't cover Monte Carlo simulation

00:48:59.260 --> 00:49:00.970
I cover a little bit about imputation.

00:49:00.970 --> 00:49:02.470
We talk about missing-data imputation.

00:49:02.470 --> 00:49:06.415
Dr. Popat also talks about that in her courses.

00:49:06.415 --> 00:49:11.095
We don't get into it in a very deep way

00:49:11.095 --> 00:49:13.950
but I would say the courses are

00:49:13.950 --> 00:49:17.395
are quite rigorous and that you are expected to not just be able to

00:49:17.395 --> 00:49:19.470
