00:49:21.430 --> 00:49:25.040
Yeah. I think we do have courses in place.

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:28.010
I teach one in the spring but it's not part of the certificate program.

00:49:28.010 --> 00:49:29.190
I think you need to,

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:30.450
as Dr. Sainani says,

00:49:30.450 --> 00:49:34.110
once you have a good foundation,

00:49:34.110 --> 00:49:39.020
the statistical techniques you have to be aware are going to keep developing.

00:49:39.020 --> 00:49:41.160
In fact we have to constantly

00:49:41.160 --> 00:49:43.950
educate ourselves about what's the new method of doing,

00:49:43.950 --> 00:49:45.605
you know, X, Y, or Z.

00:49:45.605 --> 00:49:48.890
And once we know the basics and you look up the papers and

00:49:48.890 --> 00:49:52.410
you read them and then you see the code is available

00:49:52.410 --> 00:49:53.810
Right. I teach a lot

00:49:53.810 --> 00:49:55.590
through computer simulation and I'll tell you

00:49:55.590 --> 00:49:57.555
if a new statistical method I've got to learn.

00:49:57.555 --> 00:49:59.470
I find that, "Oh, I've got to run that on my data."

00:49:59.470 --> 00:50:03.380
First thing I do is pop it into the computer and run a simulation so I have a sense of.

00:50:03.380 --> 00:50:06.675
